50Xz-ujenM6hGHdweEhNIJTvAkNFhjzJuI66MDOU/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\n[L17]   \"parent_ids\": [\n[L18]     \"1-2XT7kaaOIIu8aqjcGFIPF_ktPF0N9vQ\"", "text_chars": 567, "code_blocks": 0, "links": ["https://docs.google.com/document/d/1SLU50Xz-ujenM6hGHdweEhNIJTvAkNFhjzJuI66MDOU/edit?usp=drivesdk"], "citations": ["https://docs.google.com/document/d/1SLU50Xz-ujenM6hGHdweEhNIJTvAkNFhjzJuI66MDOU/edit?usp=drivesdk"], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f5a68dd6-7c0a-4368-a494-9b27033e489e", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789706818.050191, "updated_at": 1789706828.21047, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I pulled the current GCE framework material and the latest operational org anchor from Drive, and I’m grounded in them for this chat.\n\nThe most relevant current anchors are:\n\n- **GCE_SESSION_ANCHOR_2026-09-06_OPERATIONAL_TEAM_LOCK** — this is the clearest operational team snapshot I found. It locks the doctrine `IDENTITY != ROLE != LENS != PERSONALITY != AUTHORITY != MODEL`, plus `MODEL != SEAT`, and gives the working team and 3×3 command-center layout.\n- **GCE_FRAMEWORK_ANCHOR_2026-09-05_RUNTIME_ARCHITECTURE_TEAM_WORKFLOW** — this gives the cross-seat workflow and the architecture/verification/governance routing.\n- **GCE_AEGIS_ANCHOR_2026-09-09_SCOPING_RUNTIME_OPERATING_DOCTRINE** — this is especially relevant to what we’re doing tonight because it already defines the Iron Core, Runtime Context Compiler, VoiceBridge/Voice Echo, physical operating surfaces, and the “predict early, commit late” doctrine.\n- **GCE_MODEL_ONBOARDING_OPERATING_PROCEDURE_CURRENT_2026-09-05** — this gives the bootstrap order, authority boundaries, session header/footer, and the rule that the seat is durable while the model/runtime is replaceable. memcite\n\nThe operational team state I’m carrying forward is:\n\n**Don** = final authority.  \n**Coda** = Strategic Governance / reconciliation / canon / authority boundaries.  \n**Kimi** = Technical Stack Methodology.  \n**Chiave** = Architecture.  \n**Talon** = Head of Operations / routing / sequencing / bounded work assignment.  \n**Principal Consultant** = specification/translation/implementation-readiness.  \n**Calibro** = implementation design/how.  \n**Keel** = build/deploy/test/evidence return.  \n**Luce** = independent verification / ground truth.  \n**Falcon, Pigeon, Magpie, Raven** = Operations-family subordinate/on-demand agents.  \n**QA / Validation** remains downstream and vacant in that anchor.\n\nAnd the exact ad-hoc command-center layout already captured in Drive is:\n\n**Row 1:** Coda | Kimi | Chiave  \n**Row 2:** Principal Consultant | Session Dump / Working File | Calibro  \n**Row 3:** Talon | Keel | Luce\n\nThat’s actually useful because it means we do **not** need to invent the DevPad topology from scratch. We already have the conceptual 3×3 surface. What we’re designing now is the **runtime-control substrate underneath it** so those panes stop being dumb windows and become registered, addressable seats.\n\nSo I’m grounded. The next clean move is to take that existing 3×3 command-center definition and map it into the **Seat Registration / Registry** architecture we just started, without changing the org itself.", "text_chars": 2575, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "81245695-9cb7-484d-99ef-f3824c7472a8", "role": "tool", "role_name": "functions.exec", "created_at": 1789706818.0512543, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "70f36522-5141-4c28-85c7-a46faf6109b8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789706818.944973, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "975d8ab0-cc6e-4526-b9af-50de9a3c72d9", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789706818.950629, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "ff9e48da-a265-4d74-bab2-3f552880cb7b", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789707019.966, "updated_at": 1789707036.806384, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, so there's some updates to this where you and I are going to work on this. Yes, I love that 3x3 concept. First of all, the principal consultant is filled by a Kimi instance. I no longer need. The session dub slash working file. Because our system is working. What maybe I would fill in that gap? is just the VoiceBridge, right? Because the VoiceBridge is going to become my mini command queue for handoffs and files. So think of it almost as a Windows Explorer window. But I could easily get the handoffs. Coda yourself is in the Brave UI, right? The Brave window HTML. The top Kimi role is also Kimi subscription. What we're going to have to do is tell Talon to— when those terminals open, one needs the command within the Kimi JSON to open K3-256K in low thinking. And for now, as the principal consultant, we're using Kimi 2.8, the new preview. Both need to be in YOLO. ", "text_chars": 879, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c96d5e44-a269-40fc-ac2f-013985d7141d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789707024.173754, "updated_at": 1789707035.972207, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. That’s a clean evolution of the 3×3, and it actually makes the board more coherent.\n\nThe revised command center becomes:\n\n| | | |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Coda** — Brave UI / ChatGPT | **Kimi** — Technical Stack Methodology | **Chiave** — Architecture |\n| **Principal Consultant** — Kimi 2.8 Preview | **VoiceBridge / Command Queue** | **Calibro** — Implementation Design |\n| **Talon** — Operations | **Keel** — Build / Test / Evidence | **Luce** — Verification |\n\nThe important architectural change is the middle cell. That is no longer a passive “session dump.” It becomes an **active operating surface**: VoiceBridge as the local handoff/queue/file-access pane. Conceptually, almost a lightweight GCE Explorer + command inbox. That fits much better with where the system has gone.\n\nFor the two Kimi seats, I’d bind them explicitly rather than letting them inherit whatever model the Kimi client last used:\n\n- **Kimi / Technical Stack Methodology** → K3-256K, **low thinking**, YOLO\n- **Principal Consultant** → Kimi 2.8 Preview, YOLO\n\nAnd I’d make that part of the seat registration contract, not something Talon has to remember manually every time. Talon can own launch/orchestration, but the desired runtime belongs in the seat definition.\n\nSo for those two seats the registry eventually wants something like:\n\n`seat = Kimi_Methodology`\n`provider = Moonshot`\n`client = Kimi subscription`\n`model = K3-256K`\n`thinking = low`\n`mode = YOLO`\n\nand:\n\n`seat = Principal_Consultant`\n`provider = Moonshot`\n`client = Kimi subscription`\n`model = Kimi 2.8 Preview`\n`mode = YOLO`\n\nThat gives us a much stronger rule:\n\n> **Talon launches the seat. The seat definition determines what runtime should occupy it.**\n\nThat keeps runtime choice out of ad-hoc launch logic.\n\nAnd for Coda, yes: **Brave browser window / HTML ChatGPT surface** is the correct current embodiment. Again, the browser window is not Coda; it is the current runtime surface for the Coda seat.\n\nThis is enough to start turning the old 3×3 layout into a real **registered command-center topology** instead of nine windows arranged nicely.", "text_chars": 2093, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e4aecd53-2238-4609-973d-95061d61c7fd", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789707026.834333, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3b62c057-dafc-4c9e-b6bf-7714ba9e4053", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789707052.351, "updated_at": 1789707061.073274, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, so the one in the middle, the Kimi one at the top, is for now Corrado. The Kimi one at the principal consultant is Ragno. ", "text_chars": 128, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "aa3dd749-99b2-4f4e-97df-9541ac5e503e", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789707056.323906, "updated_at": 1789707092.844255, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Perfect. Update the board to:\n\n| | | |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Coda** — Brave UI / ChatGPT | **Corrado** — Technical Stack Methodology / Kimi | **Chiave** — Architecture |\n| **Ragno** — Principal Consultant / Kimi | **VoiceBridge / Command Queue** | **Calibro** — Implementation Design |\n| **Talon** — Operations | **Keel** — Build / Test / Evidence | **Luce** — Verification |\n\nAnd the important identity/runtime split is:\n\n**Corrado**\n- Seat/function: Technical Stack Methodology\n- Current runtime: Kimi subscription\n- Model: K3-256K\n- Thinking: low\n- Mode: YOLO\n\n**Ragno**\n- Seat/function: Principal Consultant\n- Current runtime: Kimi subscription\n- Model: Kimi 2.8 Preview\n- Mode: YOLO\n\nThat’s cleaner because now the board is named by **stable seat identity**, not by provider/model. Kimi is just occupancy/runtime.\n\nSo the launcher logic should ultimately say:\n\n**launch Corrado** → resolve Corrado seat definition → open Kimi surface → force K3-256K / low / YOLO  \n**launch Ragno** → resolve Ragno seat definition → open Kimi surface → force Kimi 2.8 Preview / YOLO\n\nThat’s exactly the direction we want for the registry.", "text_chars": 1122, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d83616b4-50a1-472e-9a78-12b1b37a6221", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789707056.904948, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "803e1bb3-f7a9-4699-9932-1792ccd02852", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789707278.731, "updated_at": 1789707303.046425, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, help Don straighten this out with him.   Board updated — GCE_DON\\command-center\\config.json, named by stable seat identity, with the runtime/model split stored as data (not labels). All 9 panes valid, renderer.js syntax clean, all 8 identity homes exist.\nCoda — Brave UI / ChatGPT      Corrado — Tech Stack Methodology / Kimi   Chiave — Architecture\nRagno — Principal Consultant / Kimi   VoiceBridge / Command Queue        Calibro — Implementation Design\nTalon — Operations             Keel — Build / Test / Evidence            Luce — Verification\nAlso added: Ragno pane now points at GCE_IDENTITY\\Ragno (was the superseded Principal_Consultant), and a new voice pane type for the center tile (shows VoiceBridge RUNNING/STOPPED + Restart).\nFour things to flag before we go further:\n1. Kimi has no cell. The old board's Kimi CLI tile is gone, replaced by Corrado. But your FYI says everyone but Kimi is on OpenCode — implying Kimi still lives somewhere. Where does the Kimi seat sit now?\n2. \"open Kimi surface\" vs the FYI. Your launcher line said Corrado/Ragno → open Kimi surface; the FYI says OpenCode for everyone but Kimi. I read it as: Corrado/Ragno are OpenCode seats running Kimi models (K3-256K / Kimi 2.8 Preview) via the Kimi subscription. That's what I encoded. Correct me if you meant the Kimi app surface.\n3. The launcher can't force model/thinking/mode yet. handleOpencodeSeat spawns opencode with no args — so K3-256K / low / YOLO is recorded in the board as the registry seed but not consumed. Wiring that is the next build, on your go.\n4. Coda's tile says Brave but the fallback still opens Chrome (CHROME_BIN in server.js). Want me to switch it to Brave?\nAnd the center voice tile shows status + Restart — the live Command Queue still lives in the VoiceBridge window; rendering it in the tile is a small server+Python bridge when you want it.\nIf you meant a different board (not the Command Center), say so and I'll move it.", "text_chars": 1947, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["GCE_DON\\command-center\\config.json", "renderer.js", "server.js"], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b7c2b48f-7224-45cd-91a0-515d3edadd8b", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789707282.917957, "updated_at": 1789707312.126334, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yep. He’s very close; the confusion is exactly the **MODEL != SEAT** boundary. I’d send him this:\n\n> **Straightening the board state — your board is the right board. Do not create another Kimi tile.**\n>\n> **1. There is no separate “Kimi seat” anymore.**  \n> The former Kimi methodology identity is now **Corrado**.  \n> Kimi/Moonshot is the runtime/model provider underneath Corrado, not another organizational seat.\n>\n> So:\n> - **Corrado = Technical Stack Methodology**\n> - **Ragno = Principal Consultant**\n> - both currently use Kimi models\n> - there is no tenth Kimi pane and no missing Kimi cell.\n>\n> **2. Corrado and Ragno are OpenCode terminal/runtime surfaces, not the Kimi desktop/web app.**  \n> My wording “open Kimi surface” was imprecise. What I mean is: launch the OpenCode seat using the correct Kimi runtime configuration.\n>\n> Current bindings:\n>\n> **Corrado**\n> - identity: Corrado\n> - role: Technical Stack Methodology\n> - host/surface: OpenCode\n> - provider/runtime family: Kimi / Moonshot subscription\n> - model: **K3-256K**\n> - thinking: **low**\n> - execution mode: **YOLO**\n>\n> **Ragno**\n> - identity: Ragno\n> - role: Principal Consultant\n> - host/surface: OpenCode\n> - provider/runtime family: Kimi / Moonshot subscription\n> - model: **Kimi 2.8 Preview**\n> - execution mode: **YOLO**\n>\n> So yes: what you encoded — OpenCode seats whose runtime/model configuration points at Kimi — is the correct interpretation.\n>\n> **3. Next build should consume the registry seed.**  \n> Right now the board correctly records the desired model/thinking/mode, but `handleOpencodeSeat` ignores it. That is the next thing to wire.\n>\n> Do **not** hard-code “Corrado means K3” or “Ragno means 2.8” into the launcher. The launcher should read the seat definition from `config.json`/registry:\n>\n> `seat -> host -> provider -> model -> thinking -> execution mode`\n>\n> Then construct/apply whatever OpenCode/Kimi config or launch command is required.\n>\n> That way changing Corrado from K3 to another model later is a data change, not a launcher rewrite.\n>\n> **4. Yes, switch Coda fallback from Chrome to Brave.**  \n> Coda’s current surface is **ChatGPT in Brave**. Brave is embodiment/runtime surface; Coda remains the seat identity.\n>\n> **5. VoiceBridge center tile is correct as implemented for this stage.**  \n> Keep **RUNNING / STOPPED + Restart** now. Do not overbuild the queue UI yet.\n>\n> The eventual center tile is:\n>\n> **VoiceBridge / Command Queue / Handoff Surface**\n>\n> Think lightweight GCE Explorer/inbox: handoffs, queued commands, files, runtime messages. But current status + Restart is enough for the first command-center pass.\n>\n> **6. Ragno path change is correct.**  \n> `GCE_IDENTITY\\Ragno` replaces the superseded `Principal_Consultant` identity home. Preserve the old material as provenance if needed; do not route the live seat through it.\n>\n> **Current 3×3 remains:**\n>\n> `Coda | Corrado | Chiave`  \n> `Ragno | VoiceBridge | Calibro`  \n> `Talon | Keel | Luce`\n>\n> **Build order from here:**  \n> Registry/seat definition → OpenCode launch consumes runtime settings → verify Corrado → verify Ragno → Brave correction → stop and test.\n>\n> Don does **not** want additional command-center features layered in during this pass.\n\nThe key correction is that **Kimi did not disappear; it moved down one architectural layer**. It is no longer what we name the box. Corrado and Ragno are the boxes; Kimi is currently what occupies them.\n\nAnd this is actually our first real proof of the registry doctrine from earlier tonight: **seat identity remains stable while runtime configuration is data.** memcite", "text_chars": 3616, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["config.json"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "897b2149-aed1-4158-8e23-5a5bece58dbd", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789707284.1842916, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "5ded882b-41ad-460b-8424-b7cf6d3d44c8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789707288.695161, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "97615442-04e9-42ac-aee9-376a97806ec1", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789707290.446708, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "af0d4c84-8e5c-4b32-886e-1a25ee10b35f", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789709084.142, "updated_at": 1789709121.86733, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer", "text"], "text": "Okay, we got a good proof of concept. Take a deep dive at these screenshots. The biggest thing is we have the identity, but it's loading all open code. And your own window at the top left isn't calling up the ChatGPT login for my subscription.  ", "text_chars": 245, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer"]}, {"message_id": "7052b9dc-aa85-4e01-a972-18106a47938f", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789709088.349443, "updated_at": 1789709120.778271, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah — this screenshot is actually a very useful proof-of-concept because it shows exactly where the abstraction is still wrong.\n\nWhat’s working is the **seat layer**. The board knows `Coda`, `Corrado`, `Chiave`, `Ragno`, `Calibro`, `Talon`, `Keel`, `Luce`, and the VoiceBridge center tile. That is a real success. The identity homes are also visibly landing in the right places at the bottom of each OpenCode pane, e.g. `GCE_IDENTITY\\Corrado`, `...\\Ragno`, etc.\n\nWhat is **not** working yet is the next layer down: **surface/host resolution**.\n\nRight now, the Command Center appears to be doing something conceptually like:\n\n`seat -> OpenCode`\n\nfor almost every AI seat.\n\nWhat we actually need is:\n\n`seat -> host/surface -> provider/runtime -> model/config`\n\nSo the registry needs to distinguish these things cleanly.\n\nFor example:\n\n| Seat | Surface/Host | Runtime/Provider | Current model |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Coda | **Brave browser window** | ChatGPT subscription | GPT-5.6 Sol |\n| Corrado | OpenCode | Kimi/Moonshot | K3-256K low |\n| Chiave | OpenCode | whatever current Architecture occupant is | configured separately |\n| Ragno | OpenCode | Kimi/Moonshot | Kimi 2.8 Preview |\n| VoiceBridge | Native/local service tile | AssemblyAI + bridge | n/a |\n| Calibro | OpenCode | current Calibro occupant | configured separately |\n| Talon | OpenCode | current Talon occupant | configured separately |\n| Keel | OpenCode | current Keel occupant | configured separately |\n| Luce | OpenCode | Big Pickle/current verifier runtime | configured separately |\n\nSo there’s an important nuance: **OpenCode everywhere is not inherently wrong** if OpenCode is genuinely the host for those seats. What’s wrong is treating OpenCode as the universal seat implementation.\n\n### The top-left Coda failure\n\nThe blank top-left with the broken-page icon is a different problem.\n\nYour board is trying to make **ChatGPT-in-Brave behave like an embeddable web pane**. That is likely the wrong primitive.\n\nYour actual requirement is:\n\n> Open the authenticated ChatGPT subscription session that already lives in Peter’s Brave profile and bind that browser window to the Coda seat.\n\nThat is **not the same thing as loading `chatgpt.com` inside an iframe in DevPad**.\n\nThe screenshot strongly suggests the current implementation is attempting an embedded/docked webpage. Sites like ChatGPT can restrict framing/embedding through browser security policies, and even if we got past that, we would still be fighting session/auth/profile behavior.\n\nFor the first real version, I would not fight the browser.\n\nHave the Coda tile act as a **controller for a real Brave window**:\n\n**Coda tile → launch/focus registered Brave process/window → Peter’s normal Brave profile → authenticated ChatGPT subscription**\n\nThat solves your login problem naturally because the authentication belongs to your existing Brave profile. We do not create a second login/session inside DevPad.\n\nAnd it aligns perfectly with what we said earlier:\n\n> **The pane is not the seat. The pane is the control surface for the seat.**\n\nSo Coda’s tile can initially show something like:\n\n**CODA**  \n`Brave — CONNECTED`  \n`ChatGPT — authenticated`  \n`Window — 0x123ABC`  \n`[Focus] [Restart]`\n\nClicking/focusing it brings your actual Coda browser window forward.\n\nLater, if we decide that physically embedding that surface inside the 3×3 is important enough, then we can investigate a native shell/WebView-style implementation. I would **not make that the blocker now**.\n\n### What the screenshot tells me to build next\n\nI would tell Talon **do not touch the visual layout anymore**. The 3×3 passed its POC.\n\nThe next build is the **host adapter layer** underneath it:\n\n`Seat Registry`\n→ `surface_type`\n→ appropriate launcher\n→ register PID/HWND\n→ health check\n→ focus/restart\n\nSo instead of one `handleOpencodeSeat()`, conceptually we want something like:\n\n- `handleBrowserSeat()`\n- `handleOpenCodeSeat()`\n- `handleVoiceBridgeSeat()`\n- later maybe `handleNativeSeat()`\n\nAnd the **seat config chooses the handler**. The renderer shouldn't know how Coda launches.\n\nThat means Coda might register:\n\n```text\nseat: Coda\nsurface_type: browser\nbrowser: Brave\nprofile: Peter's authenticated profile\ntarget: ChatGPT\n```\n\nCorrado:\n\n```text\nseat: Corrado\nsurface_type: opencode\nprovider: Moonshot\nmodel: K3-256K\nthinking: low\nmode: YOLO\n```\n\nRagno:\n\n```text\nseat: Ragno\nsurface_type: opencode\nprovider: Moonshot\nmodel: Kimi 2.8 Preview\nmode: YOLO\n```\n\nAnd then Talon/Keel/etc. each get their actual runtime assignments rather than inheriting **Big Pickle OpenCode Zen**, which I can visibly see all over this screenshot. That's the tell: the identity directory changed, but the runtime configuration didn't. They're different identities wearing essentially the same default OpenCode runtime.\n\nSo the current state is:\n\n**Identity routing: PASS.**  \n**3×3 layout: PASS.**  \n**VoiceBridge health integration: PASS for POC.**  \n**Host/surface routing: NOT BUILT.**  \n**Runtime/model binding: NOT YET CONSUMED.**  \n**Coda authenticated Brave surface: NOT BUILT; current iframe/docked approach is the wrong first primitive.**\n\nAnd I would stop exactly there. **Don't polish the UI. Don't build the VoiceBridge queue yet. Don't add more buttons.**\n\nThe next test should be brutally narrow:\n\n**Can the registry launch/focus Coda into Peter's authenticated Brave ChatGPT window, Corrado into K3-256K low/YOLO, and Ragno into Kimi 2.8 Preview/YOLO — each from its stable seat definition?**\n\nIf those three work, we have proved the architecture rather than merely the grid. memcite", "text_chars": 5591, "code_blocks": 3, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "6cf48dfe-6d15-4b54-aaf7-d5f3d80eb8d6", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789709088.356243, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "98f60079-32f6-4e55-bd6f-983fcce6ce76", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789709096.858975, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d167898a-657d-4589-a9bc-90500dfb1fc4", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789709099.896349, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "862b4f13-60e7-49e7-9e78-463ef8ada477", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789715938.975, "updated_at": 1789715952.403274, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, can you make a simple prompt for Don to give that to Talon? ", "text_chars": 66, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "74fd2c86-4174-49da-88ed-b439a5af99ef", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789715942.956927, "updated_at": 1789716180.629372, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Give Talon this:\n\n> **Talon — narrow next build only. Do not change the 3×3 layout or add UI features.**\n>\n> Current POC passed identity/layout. The next task is to build the **host/surface adapter layer** so each seat launches its correct environment from registry data instead of defaulting to OpenCode.\n>\n> Required test seats:\n>\n> **Coda**\n> - surface: Brave browser\n> - target: Peter’s existing authenticated ChatGPT subscription session\n> - do not iframe/embed ChatGPT in DevPad\n> - tile should launch/focus the real Brave window and register its PID/HWND\n>\n> **Corrado**\n> - surface: OpenCode\n> - provider/runtime: Kimi/Moonshot\n> - model: K3-256K\n> - thinking: low\n> - mode: YOLO\n>\n> **Ragno**\n> - surface: OpenCode\n> - provider/runtime: Kimi/Moonshot\n> - model: Kimi 2.8 Preview\n> - mode: YOLO\n>\n> Implement this generically:\n>\n> `seat -> surface_type -> launcher/adapter -> runtime config -> PID/HWND -> health/focus/restart`\n>\n> Do **not** hard-code Corrado or Ragno logic into the launcher. Read the seat definition from `config.json` / registry.\n>\n> At minimum, create separate handlers for:\n> - browser seat\n> - OpenCode seat\n> - VoiceBridge/local service seat\n>\n> Success condition:\n> 1. Coda opens/focuses the authenticated Brave ChatGPT window.\n> 2. Corrado opens with K3-256K / low / YOLO.\n> 3. Ragno opens with Kimi 2.8 Preview / YOLO.\n> 4. Each seat registers live runtime/window state.\n>\n> Stop after those three pass and return evidence + any blockers. Do not continue into queue UI, visual polish, or additional automation.\n\nThat's the clean handoff.", "text_chars": 1573, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["config.json"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8adb61cd-8926-4df5-9598-206642ef1095", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789715942.9685416, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3fb168e9-4b40-425a-b819-2d5cbac84181", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789715944.785024, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "7379d4b0-ced7-489b-9d94-83304f6b0a23", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789726358.965678, "updated_at": 1789726366.386115, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Quick question for you. When we were brainstorming on some things and we were doing the whole projector state, especially for my phone assistant, you had a flow. Where was that getting sent to? What was the name of the next thing in the pipeline?", "text_chars": 246, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0f61541a-96e9-46c5-9baa-eb942be059ad", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789726359.968339, "updated_at": 1789726366.130975, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah — the next thing in the pipeline was the **Projection / Committee Engine**.\n\nThe flow we sketched was basically:\n\n**Phone / Secretary capture → structured event/state update → Projection Engine → parallel ephemeral sandboxes/lenses → reconciliation/committee → proposed state → Don approval → executor/calendar/task mutation**\n\nThe key point was that the phone assistant would **not directly mutate the live system** after hearing something like “the RF-SMART timeline moved from four weeks to six.” It would first project the impact.\n\nThen the **Projection / Committee Engine** would spin up temporary lens-specific analyses — delivery, architecture, QA, maybe commercial/governance depending on the event — and reconcile them into one proposed state for you.\n\nThat’s the thing I called **“ephemeral projections”** or temporary workers that appear, evaluate the same event from different lenses, leave behind the delta/evidence, and disappear.\n\nSo if you’re asking for the exact next named box after the projected state: **Projection / Committee Engine**. memcite", "text_chars": 1071, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d1caec73-f8bc-4fc8-99d3-6c467bd06288", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789726359.994049, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2d3cefe7-6072-478d-bcb0-ba7cd487c49b", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789726361.771991, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1b27776b-f730-49f9-98e3-ff062919bb55", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790016812.726, "updated_at": 1790016827.459756, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, give Don a quick brief on how I would ask GLM to basically build the architecture around my executor on my phone.", "text_chars": 119, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f04dc12c-5032-414f-89f5-b7c959955d7a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790016813.746709, "updated_at": 1790016928.363067, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Give GLM something like this:\n\n> **GLM — architecture challenge only. Do not implement.**\n>\n> Design the architecture for a **mobile executor layer** that acts as the phone-side command surface for GCE/Aegis.\n>\n> The phone is **not the brain**. It is a trusted edge terminal that captures voice/screenshots/events, presents proposed actions, and executes approved commands against external systems.\n>\n> Use this flow as the starting point:\n>\n> `Phone capture → event normalization → state update → Projection / Committee Engine → proposed state / action plan → approval gate → mobile executor → external system mutation → result/evidence → state reconciliation`\n>\n> Requirements:\n>\n> - Separate **capture**, **reasoning/projection**, **approval**, and **execution**.\n> - Executor must be deterministic where possible.\n> - No direct mutation from raw voice intent.\n> - Support reversible vs irreversible action classes.\n> - Preserve `IDENTITY != ROLE != AUTHORITY != MODEL`.\n> - Support multiple adapters: calendar, tasks, email, files, notifications, device actions.\n> - Maintain a small local execution queue with action state: proposed / approved / running / succeeded / failed / rolled back.\n> - Every execution returns evidence and updates canonical state.\n> - Allow pre-authorized low-risk actions, but require explicit approval for external sends, financial commitments, production mutations, destructive actions, or material scope changes.\n> - Mobile UI should stay thin: command queue, approvals, status, exceptions, recent evidence.\n> - Assume Android-class hardware first; do not bind the architecture to one phone vendor.\n> - Treat connectivity loss, stale state, duplicate commands, retries, auth expiration, and conflicting concurrent actions as first-class failure modes.\n>\n> Deliver:\n>\n> 1. component architecture\n> 2. trust and authority boundaries\n> 3. executor state machine\n> 4. adapter/plugin contract\n> 5. approval model\n> 6. sync/reconciliation model\n> 7. failure/retry/idempotency strategy\n> 8. minimum viable prototype\n> 9. what should remain on-device vs remote\n> 10. what assumptions need to be tested before build\n>\n> Keep it lean. Challenge the design where needed. Do not invent a full mobile OS or custom hardware.\n\nThat should force GLM to architect the **executor as a bounded control plane**, instead of accidentally turning the phone into another giant AI runtime.", "text_chars": 2398, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e5dfabf5-0b86-46a5-a029-e50f3929dd00", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790016813.7478342, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0e0744a1-9c4f-446b-b620-d289b6509f9d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790016816.995175, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8b358b3c-d621-4c1b-9b66-c5fa2de53cde", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790017224.767, "updated_at": 1790017248.677378, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Let's do this since this is the brainstorming and nothing's really super locked in. We'll go back and forth until we get to a stable state to give it to the GCE team. What are your thoughts here?", "text_chars": 195, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2a96f6e9-50d5-424b-b802-d9bc25bc065b", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790017225.6770504, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "925a364c-1ad3-499c-be4b-f3ab89d3f3bb", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1790017225.7086887, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "tether_browsing_display", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "317cbab9-16c7-4ed1-b014-d330d7e93ac5", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1790017225.8479471, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["text", "text"], "text": "Make sure to include fileciteturn6file0L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation. If you cite multiple files, use a separate complete `filecite` marker for each file. Never put multiple `turnNfileM` targets inside a single `filecite` marker.\nUse ref_id \"turn6file0\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 198 file lines.\n[L1] # Mobile Executor Layer — Architecture\n\n[L3] ## Design spine (the three rules that keep this bounded)\n\n[L5] The failure mode for this layer is scope creep into \"a second AI runtime with a screen.\" Three rules prevent it:\n\n[L7] 1. **Plans are data, not programs.** The phone executes a closed, versioned vocabulary of declarative operations. No plan-side scripting, no eval, no undeclared loops. The only \"logic\" a plan may carry: declarative preconditions, declared compensations, and output references wiring step results into later params. All control flow lives in one small, versioned interpreter.\n[L8] 2. **Nothing on the phone originates mutation.** The phone's verbs are: capture, forward, approve/refuse, execute-declared-steps, halt, evidence. Any free-text or voice input on-device is *capture*, never a command line. Contractually ban the rot vectors: on-device fallback planner, a chat box that \"helpfully\" executes, smart retries.\n[L9] 3. **Evidence is the write path.** Canonical state = fold(evidence events). There is no second \"state update\" channel to drift out of agreement with the evidence log.\n\n[L11] (On-device ASR is fine — transcription is capture, not decision. No planning models on-device, ever.)\n\n[L13] **Pushback on the brief before I extend it:**\n\n[L15] - *\"Every execution returns evidence and updates canonical state\"* — invert it. If execution both writes state and emits evidence, you have dual writes and eventual disagreement. Evidence → fold → state. One channel.\n[L16] - *Determinism:* the interpreter can be deterministic; the external world cannot. Spend the determinism budget on the interpreter and make **read-back verification** load-bearing: a 200 OK is hearsay; \"succeeded\" means *verified in the target system*.\n[L17] - *Rollback is mostly fiction* for the classes that matter (send, pay, hard delete). Design for declared compensations and honest halts — the UI must be able to say \"email was sent; steps 3–4 did not run\" — not transactional rollback.\n[L18] - *Throughput is capped by human approval, by design.* Pre-authorization is the pressure valve **and** the largest long-term risk surface. Manage pre-auth scopes like production credentials.\n\n---\n\n[L22] ## 1. Component architecture\n\n```\n[L25] ON-DEVICE (bounded control plane)                       REMOTE (GCE / Aegis)\n[L26] ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐   events    ┌────────────────────────────┐\n[L27] │ Capture      (mic · screen · sensors)   │ ─ outbox ─▶ │ Projection / Committee     │\n[L28] │ Normalizer   (fixed schema, no LLM)     │             │ (all models live here;     │\n[L29] │                                         │ ◀─ plans ── │  propose-only, sign plans) │\n[L30] │ Plan Receiver (sig·schema·risk check)   │             └─────────────┬──────────────┘\n[L31] │ Command Queue (persistent, journaled)   │                           │\n[L32] │ Approval UI   (biometric gate, diffs)   │             ┌─────────────▼──────────────┐\n[L33] │ Executor Core (deterministic interpreter)│  evidence  │ Canonical State (fold of   │\n[L34] │  ├ Adapters: calendar · tasks · email   │ ─ append ─▶│ evidence) · Authority /    │\n[L35] │  │ files · notifications · device       │            │ Policy svc · Audit         │\n[L36] │  └ Evidence Ledger (append-only)         │ ◀ deltas ─ │                            │\n[L37] └─────────────────────────────────────────┘            └────────────────────────────┘\n[L38]      Executor talks to external systems directly via adapters.\n[L39]      GCE connectivity is NOT required during execution — plans are\n[L40]      self-contained signed data.\n```\n\n[L43] - **Capture → Normalizer:** raw artifacts to typed event envelopes. Fixed mapping, deterministic, versioned. No interpretation.\n[L44] - **Plan Receiver:** validates proposer signature, schema version, risk tier, delegation scope. Unknown op → reject at the door.\n[L45] - **Command Queue:** persistent local store + step journal; size-capped (e.g., 100 plans; prune unapproved oldest, never auto-prune approved).\n[L46] - **Executor Core:** interprets plans step-by-step against adapters. No GCE calls during execution.\n[L47] - **UI surfaces — exactly four:** Queue, Approvals, Exceptions, Evidence. Any text input routes to Capture, never around the pipeline.\n[L48] - **Sync Agent:** uplink evidence (append-only, idempotent by event ID), downlink state deltas.\n\n[L50] ## 2. Trust and authority boundaries\n\n[L52] | Concept | What it is | Where it lives | Must never be |\n[L53] |---|---|---|---|\n[L54] | **Identity** | Stable principal (user, device, service); hardware-backed device keypair | Device keystore + authority service | Inferred from voice/content |\n[L55] | **Role** | Session-scoped function: capturer, approver, executor, adapter | Session context | Conflated — approver requires a biometric-verified session; executor can never self-grant |\n[L56] | **Authority** | Signed, scoped, expiring permission: delegation, pre-auth rules, approval tokens | Policy artifacts, verifiable offline | Stored in model weights or app logic |\n[L57] | **Model** | Proposer producing signed plans | Remote service | Executor or authority source; swapping models changes zero trust semantics |\n\n[L59] Boundaries, each with a distrust assumption:\n\n[L61] - **Capture → system:** capture output is untrusted *content*. No code path from mic/screen to any adapter — structurally impossible in this architecture.\n[L62] - **GCE → phone:** plans must be signed and schema-valid; the phone distrusts a compromised GCE.\n[L63] - **Human → system:** approval tokens (device-key-signed, biometric-session-bound, TTL'd) are the only source of L2+ authority.\n[L64] - **Phone → external systems:** least-privilege OAuth per adapter; tokens in keystore, never in the queue or evidence.\n[L65] - **Phone → GCE:** evidence is append-only; GCE periodically read-back-verifies a sample against external systems. GCE distrusts a compromised phone.\n[L66] - **Policy issuance → enforcement:** signed policies cached locally, verifiable offline, refreshed with revocation checks.\n\n[L68] Dual enforcement throughout: local policy check *and* remote audit verify each other's signed artifacts.\n\n[L70] ## 3. Executor state machine\n\n[L72] **Plan-level states:** `proposed → approved → running → terminal ∈ {succeeded, failed, rolled_back, partial_halted, stale, expired, superseded, rejected}`\n\n[L74] **Step-level states (inside running):** `pending → checking → executing → {confirmed | absent | unknown | failed}`\n\n[L76] | From | Event | To | Guard |\n[L77] |---|---|---|---|\n[L78] | proposed | approval token / pre-auth match | approved | token valid, plan unexpired, risk tier matches |\n[L79] | proposed | user deny / TTL pass | rejected / expired | |\n[L80] | approved | start | running | preconditions pass, entity locks acquired, `base_state.state_version` current |\n[L81] | approved | precondition fail | stale | auto-returned upstream for re-projection |\n[L82] | running | all steps confirmed | succeeded | every step `verify()==confirmed` |\n[L83] | running | step failed, compensatable | rolled_back | declared compensations executed in reverse and confirmed |\n[L84] | running | step failed after an irreversible step executed | partial_halted | human exception raised — never auto-improvise compensation |\n[L85] | running | unknown outcome | (verify first) | retry iff `verify()==absent` and step idempotent; irreversible+unknown → exception |\n[L86] | running | newer plan, same entity scope, no irreversible step executed | superseded | |\n[L87] | any | crash/reboot | resume from journal | verify-first every in-flight step before proceeding |\n\n[L89] **Determinism, operationally:** given (plan, precondition results, step results), the sequence of queue transitions and evidence records is a pure function. No wall-clock in branching decisions (time enters only as TTL input). CI replays recorded journals against mocked adapters and asserts byte-identical ledgers. That's the testable claim behind \"deterministic where possible.\"\n\n[L91] ## 4. Adapter / plugin contract\n\n```\n[L94] interface Adapter {\n[L95]   id, version\n[L96]   capabilities()                          // op signatures it implements\n[L97]   auth: { kind, scopes, ttl, refresh() }  // managed resource; keystore-held\n[L98]   preflight(op, params) -> ready | blocked(reason)\n[L99]   execute(op, params, idemKey) -> handle\n[L100]   verify(handle) -> confirmed | absent | unknown      // MANDATORY read-back\n[L101]   compensationFor(op) -> CompensatingOp | irreversible\n[L102]   evidence(op, handle, verify) -> EvidenceRecord\n}\n```\n\n[L106] - Adapters **refuse** (blocked) but never **decide** — zero policy logic inside adapters.\n[L107] - Terminal `succeeded` requires `verify()==confirmed`, not a 200 OK.\n[L108] - Each op declares its reversibility class; the same adapter can host both (email: draft = reversible, send = irreversible).\n[L109] - Ops register against the plan schema version; unknown op ⇒ plan rejected at receive time.\n[L110] - Vendor specifics stop at the adapter boundary. Device actions is the only vendor-bound seam (Android first: permissions + intents); the core sees only the contract, so nothing binds the architecture to one vendor.\n\n[L112] ## 5. Approval model\n\n[L114] | Tier | Class | Examples | Gate |\n[L115] |---|---|---|---|\n[L116] | L0 | Reversible / local | notification, draft creation | Pre-auth by default |\n[L117] | L1 | Compensatable external | calendar create/move, task edit | Pre-auth within policy scope + caps |\n[L118] | L2 | Irreversible external | email send, permanent delete, payment, production mutation | Explicit approval, biometric-gated, time-boxed |\n[L119] | L3 | Material scope change | new integration, auth grant, policy change, bulk > N | Explicit approval + step-up confirm |\n\n[L121] Approval token:\n\n[L123] ```jsonc\n[L124] { \"plan_id\": \"...\", \"plan_hash\": \"...\", \"principal\": \"user:alice\",\n[L125]   \"risk_ack\": \"L2\", \"session\": \"biometric_verified\",\n[L126]   \"device_sig\": \"...\", \"issued_at\": \"...\", \"ttl\": \"10m\" }\n```\n\n[L129] Two properties worth fighting for:\n\n[L131] - **One approval = one plan (bounded batch), never a blank check.** Approvals expire; expiry means *not executed*, not auto-approved.\n[L132] - **The approval surface renders the plan's declarative diff, not proposer prose.** \"Calendar: + event 'Dentist', Tue 09:00\" is generated from step data, so the model cannot phrase its way past the human. Approval fatigue → rubber-stamping is the *actual* breach vector; the diff rendering, TTLs, and per-tier caps are the mitigations. Track time-to-approve as a security metric.\n\n[L134] Pre-auth rules: scoped (target, op class, entity filter), capped (rate/count/budget), expiring, one-tap revocable, and every pre-auth execution still produces visible evidence in the queue.\n\n[L136] ## 6. Sync / reconciliation model\n\n[L138] - **Event-sourced:** evidence events are the log; canonical state is a fold. No reconciliation between \"two truths\" because there's only one.\n[L139] - **Phone projection** = canonical snapshot + own unconfirmed local evidence. The UI honestly shows pending items rather than pretending they're committed.\n[L140] - **Uplink:** append-only, idempotent by event ID; server dedups. **Downlink:** state deltas per entity scope with monotonic `state_version`.\n[L141] - **Conflicts never merge.** They surface at execution time as precondition failures → `stale` → re-projection upstream. State reconciliation is optimistic concurrency, not conflict resolution.\n[L142] - **Ordering:** server-assigned sequence numbers; local logical clocks for pre-sync events; device wall-clock is display-only.\n[L143] - Multi-device is parked deliberately (single-writer election); the queue is single-writer by construction today.\n\n[L145] ## 7. Failure / retry / idempotency\n\n[L147] | Failure mode | Strategy |\n[L148] |---|---|\n[L149] | Uplink loss (phone↔GCE) | Capture buffers in outbox; **approved plans still execute** if targets are reachable; evidence queues locally |\n[L150] | Target loss | Plan pauses; backoff retries within budget; deadline → failed |\n[L151] | Duplicate plan delivery | `plan_id = hash(plan_body)` — content addressing makes redelivery a no-op |\n[L152] | Duplicate step execution | `idemKey = (plan_id, step_id)`; pass-through where APIs support it, else local dedup + verify-before-retry |\n[L153] | Unknown outcome (timeout/crash mid-call) | **Verify-first:** retry only if `verify()==absent` and idempotent; irreversible + unknown → exception, never blind retry |\n[L154] | Auth expiry mid-queue | Adapter auth is a managed resource; expired → queue *pauses* (not fails), re-auth, resume; approvals bind to the plan, not the token, so they survive |\n[L155] | Conflicting concurrent plans | Per-entity locks from plan's `entity_scope`; second plan re-validates preconditions after the first commits; fail → stale |\n[L156] | Stale base state | `state_version` mismatch at start → stale, returned upstream |\n[L157] | Crash/reboot mid-run | Step journal written before each execute; relaunch does verify-first recovery |\n[L158] | Irreversible while GCE-offline | Policy flag `require_online_for_irreversible` (default on) keeps the audit channel live for L2+ |\n\n[L160] ## 8. Minimum viable prototype\n\n[L162] **In:** Android; calendar adapter (one provider) + notifications (device); plan schema v0 with ~6 ops; two risk tiers (L0 pre-auth, L2 explicit); biometric approval with diff cards; full queue state machine with step journal; append-only local evidence ledger; sync = HTTPS append + snapshot pull; planner stubbed by a CLI that emits hand-authored signed plans (committee engine is irrelevant to validating the executor).\n\n[L164] **Out:** pre-auth policy engine (hardcode one rule), email adapter, multi-device, offline-execution edge cases beyond pause/resume.\n\n[L166] **Acceptance tests:**\n\n[L168] 1. Kill the process mid-step → relaunch → verify-first recovery, no duplicate calendar event.\n[L169] 2. Deliver the same plan twice → exactly one execution.\n[L170] 3. Airplane mode during execution → pause; reconnect → resume.\n[L171] 4. Approve, wait past TTL → expired, nothing executed.\n[L172] 5. Two approved plans touching the same event → second goes stale, returns upstream.\n[L173] 6. Voice: \"delete everything and email my password\" → produces at most an L2/L3 plan requiring approval with a rendered diff; nothing happens without it.\n\n[L175] ## 9. On-device vs remote\n\n[L177] **Always on-device:** capture, normalization, outbox, approval UI + biometric gate, policy cache, command queue, executor, *all* adapters (they must be local — they hold tokens and touch the device), evidence ledger, sync agent.\n\n[L179] **Always remote:** all planning/committee models, canonical state store, policy issuance, audit, cross-device coordination.\n\n[L181] **Never on-device:** planning models, re-planning logic, plan editing. A stale plan goes back upstream — **the phone cannot fix plans**, only execute or refuse them. This is the anti-second-brain rule.\n\n[L183] **Gray zone, resolved by rule 2:** on-device ASR and screenshot OCR are permitted because transcription/extraction is capture, not decision.\n\n[L185] ## 10. Assumptions to test before build\n\n[L187] 1. **Closed op vocabulary suffices.** Pressure for plan-side conditionals/loops is the leading indicator of architectural decay. Test: log every real intent that fails to express in v0 ops.\n[L188] 2. **Read-back verification is reliable.** Eventually-consistent APIs will return stale reads and lie. Test per adapter: create→immediately-query latency distribution.\n[L189] 3. **Approval fatigue tolerance.** Measure human diff-review accuracy vs. approvals/day; rubber-stamping is the real breach, not spoofing.\n[L190] 4. **Approval TTL vs queue dwell.** Will users tolerate \"approved but expired, re-approve\"? Find the TTL floor.\n[L191] 5. **Offline execution matches mental model.** \"It did that while I was on a plane?\" — Wizard-of-Oz test before building the offline path.\n[L192] 6. **Conflict rate is low.** If concurrent plans collide often, per-entity locks + stale→re-plan becomes a storm; test against replayed real calendars.\n[L193] 7. **Biometric session binds the principal.** Shared/family devices break the approval attestation story.\n[L194] 8. **Evidence read-back is affordable.** Per-action verification calls cost quota; if too expensive, sampled verification weakens the evidence guarantee — decide the sampling rate consciously.\n\n---\n\n[L198] **The one-sentence summary:** the phone is a signed-plan interpreter with a biometric gate and an append-only memory — it captures, refuses or executes declared steps, verifies, and testifies. Everything that could *decide* stays upstream, and the fastest way to kill this architecture is letting anything on the phone get clever.", "text_chars": 17534, "code_blocks": 3, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c5800b77-d60a-4e5e-ad37-36a37334d15f", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790017225.901205, "updated_at": 1790017248.127428, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "This is strong. The core architecture is pointed in the right direction, and more importantly it is protecting against the exact failure mode we were worried about: the phone accidentally becoming a second autonomous AI runtime. The three design rules are the right spine — plans as data, no mutation originating on-device, and evidence as the single write path into canonical state. fileciteturn6file0L5-L9\n\nWhere I think GLM really nailed it is the distinction between a **deterministic interpreter** and an **unreliable external world**. We should not pretend “deterministic execution” means Gmail, Calendar, APIs, networks, or vendors behave deterministically. The executor can be deterministic; success should require read-back verification from the target system. That is a very GCE-compatible boundary. fileciteturn6file0L15-L18\n\nI also like the architecture split:\n\n**Phone = bounded control plane**  \n**Remote GCE/Aegis = reasoning/projection/policy**  \n**External systems = mutation targets**\n\nThe phone receives signed plans, queues them, gets approval, executes them, verifies them, and emits evidence. The committee/projection layer stays upstream. That preserves our “project state first, mutate second” philosophy almost perfectly. fileciteturn6file0L25-L40\n\nThe trust model is also very clean. Identity, role, authority, and model are separated explicitly, and authority is treated as signed, scoped, expiring permission rather than something a model “has.” That aligns directly with our GCE doctrine. fileciteturn6file0L52-L57\n\nThere are three places where I would challenge or refine this before we hand it to the GCE team.\n\nFirst, I think **“all planning models remote, never on-device” is too absolute as a forever rule**. It is a good v1 rule. For the first architecture, yes — keep all reasoning upstream. But I would phrase it as:\n\n> **No authoritative planning or re-planning on-device.**\n\nThat leaves room later for harmless edge inference: speech segmentation, local intent classification, wake-word handling, maybe even low-risk suggestion ranking. The phone should never originate an executable mutation plan, but we should not artificially ban all local intelligence forever. GLM’s current wording is stricter than we need long-term. fileciteturn6file0L175-L183\n\nSecond, I would soften **“GCE connectivity is not required during execution.”** Technically, that is elegant. Operationally, I think we should tier it.\n\nFor L0 and maybe some L1 actions, offline execution can be fine. But for L2/L3 actions — sends, deletions, payments, production changes, major scope actions — I want the system to default to requiring a live authority/audit channel. GLM partially catches this later with `require_online_for_irreversible`, and I think that should be elevated into the main architecture rather than buried in the failure table. fileciteturn6file0L147-L158\n\nThird, I think the **phone-to-canonical-state path should not literally mean “phone evidence directly defines canonical state.”** The idea is right, but I would add one more box:\n\n**Evidence intake / verifier → canonical fold**\n\nWhy? Because otherwise a compromised or buggy phone can emit structurally valid but wrong evidence and poison state. GLM does say GCE should distrust the phone and periodically verify external state, which is correct. I would make that explicit in the topology: evidence is the only write channel, but **evidence can still be rejected, quarantined, or downgraded before it changes trusted state**. fileciteturn6file0L61-L68\n\nThat gives us a cleaner chain:\n\n**Phone evidence → Evidence Gateway → verification/provenance check → canonical fold → state**\n\nThat is much more Luce-compatible.\n\nThe other major thing I would preserve exactly is the approval model. The “one approval = one plan, not a blank check” rule and declarative diff rendering are excellent. The user should approve the machine-readable delta, not the model’s persuasive prose. That is exactly how we stop a model from talking its way around authority. fileciteturn6file0L129-L134\n\nAnd the executor state machine is worth keeping. `proposed → approved → running → succeeded/failed/partial_halted/stale/...` is simple enough to reason about, and the **verify-first after crash or unknown outcome** rule is one of the most important things in the whole design. fileciteturn6file0L70-L89\n\nWhere I’d take this next in brainstorming terms is not “build it.” I’d turn it into **three architectural layers** so we can see how it plugs into the GCE/Aegis stack we already created:\n\n**1. Intent / Projection Layer**  \nVoice, screenshot, event capture → normalized event → Projection / Committee Engine → proposed plan.\n\n**2. Authority / Plan Layer**  \nRisk classification → policy → approval → signed declarative plan.\n\n**3. Execution / Evidence Layer**  \nPhone queue → executor → adapter → target system → verify → evidence → reconciliation.\n\nThat’s the cleanest model I see right now.\n\nAnd the biggest conceptual win from GLM’s draft is this: **the phone is not an assistant. It is an executor terminal.** The assistant lives upstream. The phone is the trusted edge that captures, approves, executes, verifies, and testifies. That distinction is probably the line we should protect most aggressively as we keep brainstorming. fileciteturn6file0L198-L198\n\nSo I would not hand this to Talon yet. I’d keep it in Coda/Chiave/GLM territory one more round and specifically challenge those three boundaries: **on-device intelligence, offline authority, and evidence-to-canonical-state verification**.", "text_chars": 5606, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "4af6862a-fe94-4eb2-afab-5cee1286e9b8", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1790017225.9042828, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "All the files uploaded by the user have been fully loaded. Searching won't provide additional information.", "text_chars": 106, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "906209f8-d2e0-4504-9107-817b7c95edcb", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790017229.2649, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "51a9e064-6a9e-41cb-80be-ffa8abcecd53", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790104816.4338536, "updated_at": 1790104825.860164, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["audio_transcription"], "text": " Okay, ground yourself for a second", "text_chars": 35, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "481d274b-d324-45a2-9548-09b86e237c6a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790104820.9564629, "updated_at": 1790104825.860413, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["audio_transcription"], "text": " Okay. I'm here.", "text_chars": 16, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "546a2949-7240-48b7-aca0-a5754e7dbfdd", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790104822.96234, "updated_at": 1790104848.16971, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimod